<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Oskars Dankers</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1883">1883</a>–<a href="/years/1965">1965</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Oskars Dankers was a Latvian general who headed the Latvian Self-Administration, the local administration under German occupation, from 1942 to 1945. A former officer of the Russian Imperial Army, he became a general in the army of independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvia">Latvia</a> between the wars. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> he lived in exile and died in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Dankers was born on 26 March 1883 in Irlava parish in the Courland Governorate of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>. He studied at the Alexander School and the Realschule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jelgava">Jelgava</a>, graduating in 1902, and joined the 180th infantry regiment of the Russian Imperial Army stationed there. After graduating from the Vilnius Military Academy in 1906 he served with the 197th infantry regiment near Helsinki and became a Stabskapitän in 1913.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> he commanded a company and then a battalion of the 197th regiment and was promoted lieutenant colonel on 4 February 1916. He was shell-shocked and wounded several times, and was captured in July 1916 and held until 1918. On 31 May 1919 he joined the Latvian Army in Tallinn, working at the headquarters of Jorģis Zemitāns on forming new Latvian troops, then took command of a regiment shipped to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liepaja">Liepāja</a>. In November 1919 he defended Liepāja against the Bermontian Army and was made colonel.</p><p>From the end of 1919 he led the 4th Zemgale Infantry Division, with which he fought on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latgale">Latgale</a> front and which he commanded until 1933; he was promoted general on 22 June 1926 and later commanded the 1st Kurzeme Infantry Division. He was awarded the Order of Lāčplēsis, 2nd and 3rd class. Two days after the Soviet occupation in June 1940 he left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, returning when Germany went to war with the USSR in 1941 to hold high posts in the German-made Latvian Self-Administration.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In September 1944 Dankers and his family left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvia">Latvia</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> forces interned him in 1945. His posts under the German occupation brought suspicion, but no evidence of war crimes was found. Released in 1947, he lived in an UNRRA displaced persons camp, lost his sight in the 1950s, and in 1957 moved to the United States, where he published two books about his life. He died in Grand Rapids, Michigan, on 11 April 1965.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Oskars Dankers” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Latvian directors-general were appointed in 1941 to handle local affairs, and in March 1942 the structure was formalised as the Latvian Self-Administration under General <b>Oskars Dankers</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/latvian-self-administration">Latvian Self-Administration</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Irlava, in what is now <a href="/countries/latvia">Latvia</a>. Died in Grand Rapids, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=56.88,22.99&amp;year=1936" aria-label="Irlava on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5046 233 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Oskars Dankers"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5496 503h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5496 503h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=56.88,22.99&amp;year=1936">What was here in 1936? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
